% ============================================================================
% syllable-type-alias — the alternate name for the VCe syllable pattern,
% decoded from a span already sitting unused inside `silent-e-word.adj`'s
% own already-quoted source sentence (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `silent-e-word.adj`
% (`silent_e_word(word, syllable_type)`, wake/whale/while/yoke/yore/rude/
% hare → all vce_long_vowel): that table's own `source` field already
% quotes, verbatim, the Reading Rockets sentence in full --
%
%     "Also known as \"magic e\" syllable patterns, VCe syllables contain
%      long vowels spelled with a single letter, followed by a single
%      consonant, and a silent e."
%
% -- but the table's `word, syllable_type` schema only had room for WHICH
% words follow the pattern, not the sentence's OWN opening alias clause
% naming the pattern's alternate name. New `syllable_type_alias
% (syllable_type, alias)` table decodes that clause as its own row:
%
%   vce_long_vowel → magic_e
%     "Also known as \"magic e\" syllable patterns, ..."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? syllable_type_alias(vce_long_vowel, $Alias)  % magic_e
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): the single `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `syllable_type_alias(syllable_type, alias)` carrying the citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns
% the alias WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on any syllable type that is not the one tabled here (it never
% invents an alias). The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind an alias and
% recall which syllable type it names:
%
%     ? syllable_type_alias($SyllableType, magic_e)  % vce_long_vowel
%
% This is honestly a ONE-ROW table: the parent `silent-e-word.adj` tables
% only ONE syllable type (vce_long_vowel, shared across all seven of its
% rows), so there is no second already-tabled syllable type within that
% parent to use as a within-table abstention target. Instead, the parent's
% own header names the source article as Reading Rockets' "Six Syllable
% Types" -- meaning the SAME article covers six distinct syllable types in
% total, of which silent-e-word.adj tables only the one (VCe/magic e). A
% recall on `closed_syllable` -- a real syllable type the same article's
% own title implies it covers, but not one silent-e-word.adj (and
% therefore not one this sibling) tables -- correctly abstains rather than
% inventing an alias for it.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME span already quoted
% inside `silent-e-word.adj`'s own `source` field, from the SAME Reading
% Rockets "Six Syllable Types" article that table already cites -- no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a part of that
% already-verified quote. `trust consensus` -- the same tier the parent
% table already carries (Reading Rockets is a reputable literacy-education
% secondary reference, not a primary standards body).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table syllable_type_alias {
    columns syllable_type, alias

    row (vce_long_vowel, magic_e)

    source "Also known as \"magic e\" syllable patterns, VCe syllables contain long vowels spelled with a single letter, followed by a single consonant, and a silent e."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/topics/spelling-and-word-study/articles/six-syllable-types"
    trust consensus
}
